.TH STRTOD 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
strtod \- parse an ASCII floating-point value as binary64
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.BI "double strtod(const char* " text ", char** " end ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Skips ASCII whitespace and an optional sign, then parses decimal or hexadecimal
floating-point syntax, or case-insensitive infinity or NaN. The decimal point
is always a period. A valid parenthesized NaN payload is consumed but ignored.
If
.I end
is non-null, it receives the first unconsumed character, or the original
input if no conversion is performed. The caller must not modify read-only
input through that pointer.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the nearest binary64 value, ties to even, independent of the
floating-point environment. No conversion returns positive zero.
Overflow returns signed infinity and sets
.B errno
to
.BR ERANGE .
An inexact result rounded to a subnormal or zero also sets
.BR ERANGE .
Other results leave
.B errno
unchanged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR strtof (3crunch),
.BR strtold (3crunch)
